# Arbitrage Bot Project Structure

## Core Architecture
The arbitrage bot follows a modular architecture with clear separation of concerns:

- **Main Entry Point**: [main.go](mdc:main.go) - Initializes the bot, sets up websocket connections, and manages the main trading loop
- **Arbitrage Engine**: [arbitrage/arbitrage.go](mdc:arbitrage/arbitrage.go) - Core arbitrage detection and execution logic
- **Order Book Management**: [arbitrage/orderbook.go](mdc:arbitrage/orderbook.go) - Real-time order book data handling and triangle detection
- **Currency System**: [currency/pairs.go](mdc:currency/pairs.go) - Currency pair definitions and management
- **Exchange Interface**: [exchange.go](mdc:exchange.go) - Binance API integration and order execution
- **Configuration**: [utils/config.go](mdc:utils/config.go) - Environment-based configuration management
- **Logging**: [logger/logger.go](mdc:logger/logger.go) - Structured logging with color support

## Key Components

### Triangle Arbitrage
- **Triangle Structure**: Three sequential trades forming a closed loop (e.g., USDC → BTC → ETH → USDC)
- **Path Objects**: Each leg of the triangle with direction (BUY/SELL) and price data
- **Arbitrage Detection**: Real-time profit calculation using order book data
- **Execution Engine**: Atomic trade execution with rollback capabilities

### Order Book Management
- **Concurrent Maps**: Thread-safe symbol storage using `cmap.ConcurrentMap`
- **Atomic Values**: Lock-free price updates using `atomic.Value`
- **WebSocket Integration**: Real-time price feeds from Binance
- **Triangle Discovery**: Automatic detection of profitable trading triangles

### Configuration System
- **Environment Variables**: All settings loaded from `.env` file
- **Simulation Mode**: Safe testing without real trades
- **Fee Management**: Configurable trading fees and minimum amounts
- **Asset Selection**: Configurable base currencies and starting assets

## File Organization
- Keep related functionality in the same package
- Use descriptive package names that reflect their purpose
- Separate concerns: trading logic, data structures, utilities, and external integrations
- Maintain clear interfaces between packages